                "text": "Read file C:\\Users\\nashw\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\out.txt, lines 1-307\n1: Semester Project: Test-Driven and Model-Based Testing of a Ticket\n2: Booking System\n3: Course: Software Testing (2010031410)\n4: Project Weight: 30 points\n5: Project Type: Group project, 2–3 students\n6: Duration: One semester\n7: Delivery: Two milestones\n8: Course Learning Outcomes Assessed: CLO2, CLO4, and CLO6\n9: Part I: General Project Information\n10: 1. Project Purpose and Scope\n11: Each group will develop and test a small Ticket Booking System. The project combines Test-Driven\n12: Development (TDD), test doubles and mocking, model-driven test design, Input Space Partitioning (ISP), pair-\n13: wise coverage, and automated parameterized testing.\n14: The emphasis is on the quality of the testing process, not on building a large application. The project uses\n15: one repository throughout the semester. Milestone 2 extends the system and tests developed in Milestone 1.\n16: A graphical user interface, database, REST API, authentication system, deployment, or real external service\n17: integration is not required.\n18: 2. Required Technology\n19: Use the following unless the instructor approves an equivalent setup:\n20: Java\n21: JUnit 5\n22: Mockito\n23: Maven or Gradle\n24: Git and GitHub\n25: The project must compile and execute its tests from the command line using the command documented in\n26: README.md.\n27: 3. Teamwork and Repository Requirements\n28: Each group must use one GitHub repository for both milestones.\n29: Each student must make meaningful commits through their own GitHub account.\n30: Commit messages should describe the work performed.\n31: The repository history must show genuine project progress. Artificial commits created only to simulate\n32: TDD or individual participation are not acceptable.\n33: The final commit hash for each milestone must be recorded in the submission manifest.\n34: A Team Contribution Matrix must identify each member’s main responsibilities and contributions.\n35: 4. Responsible Use of Generative AI\n36: Software_Testing_Project.md\n37: 1 / 8\n38: \n39: -- 1 of 8 --\n40: \n41: Generative AI and coding assistants may be used as supporting tools. Students remain responsible for every\n42: submitted test, expected result, design decision, source file, and statement in the report.\n43: Permitted use\n44: AI may be used to:\n45: clarify course concepts, APIs, syntax, and error messages;\n46: suggest initial test cases, code or test skeletons, input blocks, or refactoring ideas;\n47: review code readability or improve report language; and\n48: support troubleshooting.\n49: Required disclosure and verification\n50: Material AI assistance affecting assessed code, tests, test data, models, or report analysis must be summarized\n51: in the AI Use Log. Minor autocomplete, formatting, and grammar corrections do not require individual\n52: entries.\n53: Students must review, execute, and independently verify AI-assisted output. AI-generated output must not be\n54: accepted only because it compiles or passes.\n55: Prohibited use\n56: Students must not:\n57: submit work they cannot explain or modify;\n58: use AI to fabricate Git history, TDD stages, test results, coverage, references, or contribution evidence;\n59: conceal material AI assistance;\n60: upload credentials, personal data, confidential material, or another student’s work to an AI service; or\n61: use AI to replace the group’s own analysis and decision-making.\n62: Every submission must include an AI Use Declaration, including when no AI was used. AI use itself does not\n63: earn marks. Marks are awarded for testing quality, critical verification, transparency, and achievement of the\n64: CLOs.\n65: 5. Student Support Materials\n66: The instructor will provide:\n67: a starter repository with JUnit 5, Mockito, and build configuration;\n68: report, submission-manifest, contribution-matrix, and AI-log templates;\n69: a submission checklist;\n70: command-line build and test instructions; and\n71: a short TDD example unrelated to the assessed system.\n72: These materials support setup and submission but do not provide solutions to the assessed requirements.\n73: Part II: Milestone 1\n74: 6. Milestone 1: TDD, Testable Design, and Mocking\n75: Software_Testing_Project.md\n76: 2 / 8\n77: \n78: -- 2 of 8 --\n79: \n80: Topics: Test-Driven Development, dependency injection, test doubles, mocking, and automated unit testing.\n81: 6.1 Scenario and Required Design\n82: Implement a BookingManager that processes a BookingRequest.\n83: A BookingRequest contains:\n84: customerId: identifies the customer;\n85: eventId: identifies the requested event;\n86: ticketQuantity: number of requested tickets; and\n87: pricePerTicketInCents: positive integer representing the price of one ticket in the smallest currency\n88: unit.\n89: For example, 2500 represents 25.00 currency units. The amount charged in Milestone 1 is:\n90: totalAmountInCents = ticketQuantity × pricePerTicketInCents\n91: A request is valid when:\n92: customerId is not null, empty, or blank;\n93: eventId is not null, empty, or blank;\n94: ticketQuantity is between 1 and 5, inclusive; and\n95: pricePerTicketInCents is greater than zero.\n96: BookingManager must receive the following dependencies through constructor dependency injection:\n97: IPaymentGateway: processes a payment and returns a nonblank transaction ID when successful. A\n98: failed payment must be represented by a documented exception or failure result.\n99: INotificationService: sends a booking confirmation.\n100: IEventRepository: checks whether an event is sold out and records a successfully paid booking.\n101: The required repository operations are:\n102: isSoldOut(eventId): determines whether the event is sold out; and\n103: saveBooking(booking): records a successfully paid booking.\n104: “Records a booking” means invoking saveBooking(...); a real database or file-storage implementation is\n105: not required. Payment, notification, and repository dependencies must be replaced with test doubles in the\n106: unit tests.\n107: 6.2 Required Behaviors\n108: M1-US1: Successful booking\n109: Given a valid request, when the event is not sold out and payment succeeds:\n110: IEventRepository.isSoldOut(eventId) is invoked once;\n111: IPaymentGateway.processPayment(...) is invoked once with the calculated total amount;\n112: IEventRepository.saveBooking(...) is invoked once;\n113: Software_Testing_Project.md\n114: 3 / 8\n115: \n116: -- 3 of 8 --\n117: \n118: INotificationService.sendConfirmation(...) is invoked once; and\n119: a successful result containing the transaction ID is returned.\n120: M1-US2: Invalid request\n121: A request is invalid when any validity condition in Section 6.1 is not satisfied. The request must be rejected\n122: before any external dependency is used.\n123: For an invalid request:\n124: IEventRepository.isSoldOut(...) must not be invoked;\n125: IPaymentGateway.processPayment(...) must not be invoked;\n126: IEventRepository.saveBooking(...) must not be invoked; and\n127: INotificationService.sendConfirmation(...) must not be invoked.\n128: A JUnit 5 parameterized test using representative invalid values is sufficient.\n129: M1-US3: Sold-out event\n130: Given a valid request, when the event is sold out:\n131: IEventRepository.isSoldOut(eventId) is invoked once;\n132: payment, saveBooking(...), and confirmation must not be invoked; and\n133: a sold-out result is returned or a documented exception is thrown.\n134: M1-US4: Failed payment\n135: Given a valid request and an event that is not sold out, when payment fails:\n136: IEventRepository.isSoldOut(eventId) is invoked once;\n137: IPaymentGateway.processPayment(...) is invoked once;\n138: IEventRepository.saveBooking(...) and confirmation must not be invoked; and\n139: a payment-failure result is returned or a documented exception is thrown.\n140: 6.3 TDD Evidence\n141: For at least two required behaviors, the repository and report must identify a credible Red–Green–Refactor\n142: progression:\n143: a failing test is added;\n144: the minimum implementation makes it pass; and\n145: the code or test is improved while the tests remain passing.\n146: Commit references are sufficient. Videos and screenshots of each step are not required.\n147: 6.4 Milestone 1 Deliverables\n148: The repository must include the required production interfaces and classes, BookingManagerTest, build\n149: configuration, README.md, and the milestone report.\n150: The Milestone 1 report should contain:\n151: Software_Testing_Project.md\n152: 4 / 8\n153: \n154: -- 4 of 8 --\n155: \n156: brief design overview;\n157: explanation of dependency injection and testability;\n158: TDD evidence for at least two behaviors using commit references;\n159: a short analysis of one limitation or risk of mocking; and\n160: Team Contribution Matrix.\n161: Recommended report length: 3–5 pages, excluding the title page and appendices.\n162: 6.5 Milestone 1 Rubric\n163: Criterion \tCLO Points\n164: Testable design and constructor dependency injection \tCLO2 \t3\n165: TDD process and repository evidence \tCLO4 \t3\n166: Automated unit tests, meaningful stubbing, and interaction verification CLO4 \t6\n167: Analysis of mocking limitations \tCLO4 \t1\n168: Collaboration and contribution evidence \tCLO6 \t2\n169: Total \t15\n170: Part III: Milestone 2\n171: 7. Milestone 2: Model-Driven Test Design, ISP, and Parameterized Testing\n172: Topics: Model-Driven Test Design, Input Space Partitioning, constraints, pair-wise coverage, and\n173: parameterized testing.\n174: 7.1 How Milestone 2 Extends Milestone 1\n175: Milestone 2 extends the same Ticket Booking System. Students must retain the Milestone 1 classes and tests,\n176: then add a DiscountCalculator to calculate the discount applied before BookingManager requests\n177: payment.\n178: BookingRequest is extended with the following customer information:\n179: customerType: NEW, REGULAR, or PREMIUM;\n180: totalOrdersInLastYear: integer; and\n181: subscribedToNewsletter: true or false.\n182: DiscountCalculator returns the final discount as an integer percentage from 0 to 15. BookingManager uses\n183: it to calculate:\n184: subtotalInCents = ticketQuantity × pricePerTicketInCents\n185: discountInCents = subtotalInCents × discountPercentage ÷ 100\n186: finalAmountInCents = subtotalInCents − discountInCents\n187: Software_Testing_Project.md\n188: 5 / 8\n189: \n190: -- 5 of 8 --\n191: \n192: Integer division is used when calculating discountInCents; any fractional cent is discarded. For example, a\n193: subtotal of 505 cents with a 7% discount gives a discount of 35 cents and a final amount of 470 cents.\n194: The Milestone 1 booking workflow remains unchanged except that\n195: IPaymentGateway.processPayment(...) must now receive finalAmountInCents rather than the\n196: undiscounted total. Existing Milestone 1 tests must remain passing and be updated only where the expected\n197: payment amount is affected by this extension.\n198: The main assessed work in Milestone 2 is the model-driven design and automated testing of\n199: DiscountCalculator. A separate integration-test suite is not required. One focused BookingManager test\n200: confirming that the calculated final amount is passed to the payment gateway is sufficient.\n201: 7.2 Discount Rules\n202: DiscountCalculator accepts:\n203: customerType;\n204: totalOrdersInLastYear; and\n205: subscribedToNewsletter.\n206: It applies these rules:\n207: Every valid customer receives a 5% base discount.\n208: Newsletter subscribers receive an additional 2%.\n209: Customer-type bonus: NEW 0%, REGULAR 3%, and PREMIUM 5%.\n210: A customer with 10 or more orders receives an additional 5% loyalty bonus.\n211: NEW with 10 or more orders is infeasible and must cause IllegalArgumentException.\n212: A negative order count is invalid and must cause IllegalArgumentException.\n213: A null customer type is invalid and must cause IllegalArgumentException.\n214: The final discount cannot exceed 15%.\n215: 7.3 Required Test Design Work\n216: Students must:\n217: define disjoint and complete blocks for customer type, order-count range, and newsletter subscription;\n218: use the order-count blocks: less than 0, 0–9, and 10 or more;\n219: document the infeasible NEW/loyal combination and test invalid inputs separately;\n220: create a concise pair-wise set covering all feasible pairs of valid blocks;\n221: identify excluded infeasible combinations and remove redundant rows where possible;\n222: automate the feasible rows using one JUnit 5 parameterized test;\n223: add focused tests for invalid inputs, the infeasible combination, and the 15% cap where needed; and\n224: add one BookingManager test verifying that the discounted final amount is passed to\n225: IPaymentGateway.\n226: A full exhaustive Cartesian-product suite is not required.\n227: 7.4 Milestone 2 Deliverables\n228: Update the same repository to include DiscountCalculator, DiscountCalculatorTest, the extended\n229: booking request or equivalent customer information, the updated BookingManager tests, the updated\n230: Software_Testing_Project.md\n231: 6 / 8\n232: \n233: -- 6 of 8 --\n234: \n235: README.md, and the final cumulative report.\n236: The final report should contain:\n237: brief system and test-scope overview;\n238: revised Milestone 1 design, TDD evidence, and mocking analysis;\n239: a short explanation of how DiscountCalculator extends the booking workflow;\n240: ISP characteristics and blocks;\n241: constraints and infeasibility justification;\n242: final pair-wise table and short coverage justification;\n243: short analysis of pair-wise testing limitations; and\n244: updated Team Contribution Matrix.\n245: Recommended final report length: 6–9 pages, excluding the title page and appendices.\n246: 7.5 Milestone 2 Rubric\n247: Criterion \tCLO Points\n248: ISP characteristics and disjoint/complete blocks \tCLO2 \t3\n249: Pair-wise model, feasible coverage, and constraints \tCLO2 \t4\n250: Correct parameterized, focused, and booking-integration tests CLO4 \t5\n251: Analysis of pair-wise testing limitations \tCLO4 \t1\n252: Collaboration and contribution evidence \tCLO6 \t2\n253: Total \t15\n254: Part IV: Submission Requirements\n255: 8. Moodle Submission Package\n256: For each milestone, submit one ZIP file through Moodle containing:\n257: repository.bundle\n258: report.pdf\n259: submission-manifest.txt\n260: ai-use-log.pdf \t(only when material AI use is declared)\n261: repository.bundle must contain the complete Git repository and its history.\n262: report.pdf must be the report version required for that milestone.\n263: submission-manifest.txt must identify the students, milestone, final commit hash, build/test\n264: command, test status, contribution declaration, and AI Use Declaration.\n265: The AI Use Log may be included as a report appendix instead of a separate file.\n266: The repository bundle and final commit hash recorded in the manifest constitute the authoritative repository\n267: evidence. A GitHub link must also be provided, but it does not replace the Moodle submission.\n268: Software_Testing_Project.md\n269: 7 / 8\n270: \n271: -- 7 of 8 --\n272: \n273: Before submission, students must verify that the ZIP opens, the repository bundle can be restored, and the\n274: project builds and executes its tests from the command line.\n275: 9. Submission and Quality Rules\n276: Submit the repository URL and ZIP package through the relevant Moodle milestone activity.\n277: Do not include compiled output, dependency folders, IDE build folders, credentials, access tokens, or\n278: unrelated files.\n279: Tests that do not compile or execute cannot receive full automation marks.\n280: Missing or unusable evidence may affect the related rubric criteria.\n281: Minor naming or folder differences are acceptable when the project remains clear and executable.\n282: Academic-integrity concerns will be handled according to University policy.\n283: Students may be asked to briefly explain or modify selected parts of their submission to verify\n284: understanding and contribution.\n285: 10. Workload Boundaries\n286: Required\n287: BookingManager, DiscountCalculator, and small supporting interfaces or domain objects;\n288: automated JUnit 5 and Mockito tests;\n289: two documented TDD cycles;\n290: one ISP/pair-wise model and its parameterized automation;\n291: one focused test connecting the discount calculation to the booking payment amount;\n292: one cumulative report;\n293: repository and collaboration evidence; and\n294: transparent declaration of material AI assistance.\n295: Not required\n296: GUI or mobile interface;\n297: web application or REST API;\n298: real database, payment, email, or SMS integration;\n299: deployment, containers, or cloud hosting;\n300: performance, load, security, or mutation testing;\n301: exhaustive combinations; or\n302: a presentation or demonstration video unless announced separately.\n303: Software_Testing_Project.md\n304: 8 / 8\n305: \n306: -- 8 of 8 --\n307: "
